{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-87153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The amount of water per month consumed by a randomly selected person\n- The number of pages in a particular book\n- The amount of time that a randomly selected person spends waiting for a particular bus" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of pages in a particular book is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-87153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The score when a roulette wheel is spun at random\n- The amount of water consumed by a randomly selected person on a particular day, measured in liters\n- The number of students in a particular class on a randomly selected day" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible outcomes do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-87155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-86911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The percent concentration of fruit concentrate in a randomly selected fruit drink\n- The number of times a coin is flipped until it lands on tails\n- The number of days in a particular week" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-86911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the concentration can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of days in a particular week is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $7$ days in a week.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-86911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-86911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac