h of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-87160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- The number of hairs on a cat" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/819fa17f44f669746ba0c82d2d986f1cd5955a1d7d8144eda6922c0cb7813c9c.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/6e540dc9309f2a5d1753760b4050c049e380705914d7289b47319de0b0696a9a.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{3}-1),\\, & \\frac{1}{2}<x\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some value of $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}4({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x]}_{1/2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{81}{64}-\\frac{3}{2}]-[\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{80}{64}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/0c30c392223857c77cfc8619f1fa0d4b7fca20af618bdd424570cc3ed712f38b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1,\\, & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[9-3]-[1-1] \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}},\\, & 3<x<6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}k{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{72}+\\frac{k}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{24}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-87236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{24}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-87236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k-x,\\, & -1<x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-[-k-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =k+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k(x+1),\\, & 2<x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =k{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =k([\\frac{9}{2}+3]-[\\frac{4}{2}+2]) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{9}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c4a186e5aecfa61e70d4fe50418f4589eebd79ebb36f78dff23bdee1ce36e317"} {"question" "q-158442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "q-158442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba069f62b9b6b8338ebd0a68c71cdae1c60fb1178b754c1cf850bcfac16e9512"} {"question" "e-10837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n\n- The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n\n- The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10837.json" "file_sha256" "d3c729e73c0bdd47ba0be4124a77c6a1854dcb1899de7517ef57159d40e7f660"} {"question" "e-10837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10837.json" "file_sha256" "d3c729e73c0bdd47ba0be4124a77c6a1854dcb1899de7517ef57159d40e7f660"} {"question" "e-10838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10838.json" "file_sha256" "8c60053cfc30373dcc179bf6986a31e4dd84a9ff32aba2860b62bb4c735cb7cc"} {"question" "e-10838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10838.json" "file_sha256" "8c60053cfc30373dcc179bf6986a31e4dd84a9ff32aba2860b62bb4c735cb7cc"} {"question" "e-11037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-11037.json" "file_sha256" "353e69aff50adfd4d1657e0b2e8dc5e13096ff8e2e6b209a40eb5e92bbf2146e"} {"question" "e-11037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-11037.json" "file_sha256" "353e69aff50adfd4d1657e0b2e8dc5e13096ff8e2e6b209a40eb5e92bbf2146e"}] "usage" {"q-98912" [] "q-86911" [] "e-11037" [] "e-10837" [] "e-10838" []} "basis" 1401 "retractions" [[17592186106456 :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n1. The percent concentration of fruit concentrate in a randomly selected fruit drink\n2. The number of times a coin is flipped until it lands on tails\n3. The number of days in a particular week"] [17592186106473 :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{k}{x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\le x \\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$."] [17592186105367 :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n1. The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n2. The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n3. The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$"] [17592186105367 :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/0018f659e35a580657f383181bfabb0d.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots$, which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty)$.\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $[MATH: (0, 1\\,000].]$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186105368 :question/problem "Consider 